---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Term from "@components/Term.astro";
import { ROLES } from "@data/roles";
import { GLOSSARY } from "@data/glossary";
const BASE = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, ""); // "/vrx" — prefix raw /img/ refs
---
<Layout>
  <!-- ─────────────────────────── HERO ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="hero" style="padding-top:24px;">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="seal">Vetted</span>
      <h1 style="margin-top:18px;">
        The trusted, global marketplace<br />for unique assets.
      </h1>
      <p class="lead" style="max-width:760px;">
        <strong style="color:var(--text)">VRX</strong> is a private,
        <Term k="on-chain">on-chain</Term>
        <Term k="primitive">reservation primitive</Term> — one
        <Term k="source-of-truth">source of truth</Term> for who reserved what. An operator with
        unique stock feeds live availability to many mutually-distrusting sales teams, who
        <strong style="color:var(--text)">view → book → reserve → settle</strong> — privately,
        provably, and without ever exposing a name, a price, or a deal term to the public.
      </p>
      <div style="display:flex;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin:26px 0 10px;">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#contact">Join the private alpha</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="#how">See how it works</a>
      </div>
      <div class="status-row" style="margin-top:8px;">
        <span class="dot live"></span> Live on test network, with the full product built &amp; demo-able today
        &nbsp;·&nbsp; private alpha opening soon
      </div>

      <!-- NEAR hackathon strip — timely, honest; links to the public repo + directory + whitepapers. -->
      <div style="margin-top:20px;padding:14px 18px;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:12px;background:var(--panel);display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px 20px;align-items:center;">
        <span class="seal">NEAR Hackathon 2026</span>
        <span style="color:var(--dim);font-size:0.95rem;">Building VRX in the open —</span>
        <a href="https://github.com/DUST-CHAIN/vrx-protocol" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Public code&nbsp;↗</a>
        <a href="https://nearbuilders.org/builders/vetted.near" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NEAR Builders&nbsp;↗</a>
        <a href="./whitepapers/">Whitepapers&nbsp;↗</a>
      </div>

      <figure style="margin-top:44px;">
        <img
          src={`${BASE}/img/hero-ecosystem.jpg`}
          alt="The Vetted Realty ecosystem: a developer's node feeds verified availability on-chain, synced globally to developers, agencies, agents and buyers."
          width="1600" height="900"
        />
        <figcaption>
          The ecosystem at a glance: a developer's <Term k="node">node</Term> publishes verified
          availability on-chain; <Term k="regional-node">regional nodes</Term> keep it in sync
          worldwide; vetted developers, agencies, agents and buyers all read one live truth.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── WHAT IT IS ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="what">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">What it is</p>
      <h2>A single, honest record that no one can quietly rewrite.</h2>
      <p class="lead" style="max-width:820px;">
        Most markets for high-value, one-of-a-kind items run on mutually-distrusting copies: the
        developer's spreadsheet, each agency's own list, the agent's memory. They drift, they
        double-sell, and disputes take months. VRX replaces all of that with one shared record
        everyone can trust — and still keeps every sensitive detail private.
      </p>

      <div class="grid cols-3 reveal reveal-stagger" style="margin-top:32px;">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>One source of truth</h3>
          <p>
            Availability lives in one place that every permitted party reads live. No stale lists,
            no two agents selling unit&nbsp;312 at once.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Private by design</h3>
          <p>
            Every real-world identifier — who, which unit, what price — is stored only as a sealed
            <Term k="hash">fingerprint</Term>. The chain shows "available / held / settled", never
            the deal. <Term k="privacy-moat">Privacy is designed in</Term>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Provable forever</h3>
          <p>
            Each agreement is time-stamped and tamper-proof. A deal struck today is exactly as
            provable years later — <Term k="provenance">full provenance</Term>, no paper to lose.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p style="margin-top:28px;max-width:860px;">
        Reservation capacity is itself a scarce, tamper-evident <strong style="color:var(--text)">booking
        token</strong> that passes down the trust chain — developer to agency to agent — so an agent can
        secure a unit and take a deposit, with every hand-off provable. Inside the system, an
        <strong style="color:var(--text)">in-system $VRX credit</strong> (not fiat, not a tradeable coin)
        meters and settles activity. Both are live in the product today.
      </p>

      <p style="margin-top:20px;max-width:860px;">
        It began as the engine behind a decade of real property sales, and it is built to be
        <strong style="color:var(--text)">domain-agnostic</strong>: at its core it doesn't "know" it's
        real estate. It fits <strong style="color:var(--text)">any high-value item that needs no
        shipping</strong> — the thing changes hands by deed, key, or access, not a courier:
        land, houses and condos · fine art · event and travel tickets · software licence keys ·
        AI models and agents · and more we haven't folded in yet. Each is a
        <Term k="non-substitutable">unique, non-substitutable</Term> asset. Real estate is the
        proof; the primitive is the product.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── HOW IT WORKS ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="how">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
      <h2>From the developer's office to a global marketplace — in one flow.</h2>

      <figure style="margin:26px 0 34px;">
        <img
          src={`${BASE}/img/how-it-works.jpg`}
          alt="Three-panel flow: the developer's office publishes availability; it is verified on-chain; vetted users transact in the marketplace."
          width="1600" height="640"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Developer materials → the developer's <Term k="node">node</Term> → verified by VRX
          <Term k="on-chain">on-chain</Term> → synced via <Term k="regional-node">regional nodes</Term>
          → vettedrealty.com users.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="grid cols-3 reveal reveal-stagger">
        <div class="card raised">
          <p class="chip">step 1</p>
          <h3 style="margin-top:10px;">Publish</h3>
          <p>
            A <Term k="developer">developer</Term> runs their own secure node and publishes live
            availability for their units. Only availability goes out — prices, buyers and milestones
            stay sealed and private.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card raised">
          <p class="chip">step 2</p>
          <h3 style="margin-top:10px;">Reserve</h3>
          <p>
            A vetted <Term k="agent">agent</Term> browses the live truth, requests to book a specific
            unit for a buyer, and chats directly with the decision-maker. Agreed units are placed on
            a <Term k="hold">hold</Term> so no one else can take them.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card raised">
          <p class="chip">step 3</p>
          <h3 style="margin-top:10px;">Settle</h3>
          <p>
            Deposit and <Term k="settlement">settlement</Term> complete the deal. Amounts stay
            private; the shared record simply moves the unit from held to settled — provable to every
            permitted party, invisible to everyone else.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card" style="margin-top:28px;">
        <h3>Why "trusted <em>and</em> trustless"?</h3>
        <p>
          <Term k="vetted">Vetted</Term> means every participant is verified before they can act — a
          network of known, accountable parties. <Term k="trustless">Trustless</Term> means you still
          don't have to <em>rely</em> on anyone's goodwill, because the record itself enforces the
          rules and proves what happened. You get the comfort of a vetted club <strong style="color:var(--text)">and</strong>
          the certainty of math.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── ROLES (teaser → /for-you) ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="roles">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">For you</p>
      <h2>Read it from your own side of the table.</h2>
      <p class="lead" style="max-width:760px;">
        Whoever you are in the deal, VRX looks different — and works for you. Pick your role to see what
        it is, how it works, how you'll use it, and why you can trust it.
      </p>

      <div class="grid cols-3 reveal reveal-stagger" style="margin-top:26px;">
        {ROLES.map((r) => (
          <a class="card" href={`./for-you.html#${r.id}`} style="text-decoration:none;border-left:3px solid var(--accent);display:block;">
            <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap;">
              <h3 style="margin:0;">{r.name}</h3>
              {r.tag === "coming soon"
                ? <span class="status-row"><span class="dot soon"></span>Planned</span>
                : <span class="chip">{r.tag}</span>}
            </div>
            <p style="margin:10px 0 0;">{r.tagline}</p>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>

      <p style="margin-top:22px;">
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="./for-you.html">See all roles in detail →</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── TRUST / MOAT ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="trust">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Why it's safe</p>
      <h2>The hard guarantees — stated plainly.</h2>
      <div class="grid cols-2 reveal reveal-stagger" style="margin-top:28px;">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Nothing sensitive is ever public</h3>
          <p>
            No name, no price, no deal term, no payment ever touches the public chain. They live
            off-chain as <Term k="hmac">keyed seals</Term> — fingerprints only the key-holder can
            verify. The world sees that a unit is reserved; it can never see the deal.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>No one can rewrite history</h3>
          <p>
            Once an agreement or reservation is sealed <Term k="on-chain">on-chain</Term>, it cannot
            be secretly altered or back-dated — not by a counterparty, not by us. Disputes get short
            because the record is the referee.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Every item is specific</h3>
          <p>
            VRX treats inventory as <Term k="non-substitutable">non-substitutable</Term>: unit 312 is
            not unit 311. You reserve <em>the</em> thing, and its value tracks the specific thing —
            no bait-and-switch, no quiet substitution.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Everyone is vetted</h3>
          <p>
            Participation requires verification. Identity is checked once, sealed, and reusable — so
            the whole network is made of known, accountable parties. <Term k="trustless">Trustless</Term>
            safety on top of a <Term k="vetted">vetted</Term> membership.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── VISION / PUBLIC GOOD ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="vision">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">The bigger picture</p>
      <h2>A missing piece of public market infrastructure.</h2>
      <p class="lead" style="max-width:840px;">
        Every economy has markets for unique, high-value things — land, homes, equipment, livestock,
        rights, slots. Almost all of them still run on private, unverifiable, easily-disputed records.
        A neutral <Term k="primitive">reservation primitive</Term> — one that lets any vetted party
        reserve any unique thing, privately and provably — is infrastructure the whole economy is
        missing.
      </p>
      <p style="max-width:840px;">
        VRX is that primitive, proven first in real estate and designed from the ground up to be
        domain-agnostic. It's built on the principles the best public-interest technology is converging
        on — <strong style="color:var(--text)">local-first, private by design, and neutral</strong>:
        your data and keys stay on your own machine, sensitive details never leave it, and no single
        party owns the rails. Built this way, it could let anyone who is vetted — anywhere — transact
        unique inventory with the same certainty today reserved for the largest institutions. That is
        the constitutional idea behind it:
        <strong style="color:var(--text)">the freedom to transact, globally, with proof instead of trust in a middleman.</strong>
      </p>
      <div class="grid cols-2 reveal reveal-stagger" style="margin-top:28px;">
        <div class="card raised">
          <h3>Proven, not theoretical</h3>
          <p>This is not a whitepaper. The engine has run real property sales for over a decade; VRX hardens and generalises it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card raised">
          <h3>Domain-agnostic by design</h3>
          <p>The core doesn't know it's real estate. The same primitive serves any high-value asset that needs no shipping — land, art, tickets, licence keys, AI software — any unique inventory.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card raised">
          <h3>Local-first &amp; private by design</h3>
          <p>Data, keys and a local privacy model live on the operator's own machine. Personal details are redacted and sealed locally before anything is shared — privacy isn't a policy, it's the architecture.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card raised">
          <h3>Built to be shared</h3>
          <p>Designed as neutral infrastructure a whole ecosystem of vetted operators can build on — not a walled garden.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── ROADMAP ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="roadmap">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Roadmap</p>
      <h2>What's live now, and what's coming.</h2>
      <p class="lead" style="max-width:760px;">
        We're building in the open. Here's an honest picture of where things stand.
      </p>
      <div class="grid cols-2 reveal reveal-stagger" style="margin-top:28px;">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="status-row" style="margin-bottom:12px;"><span class="dot live"></span> Available now</div>
          <ul class="ticks">
            <li>The core <Term k="primitive">reservation primitive</Term> — live on test network, battle-tested lineage.</li>
            <li>Working role portals for developers, agencies and agents.</li>
            <li>Private, sealed availability, holds and agreements with <Term k="provenance">full provenance</Term>.</li>
            <li>Signed sale &amp; purchase contracts, payment ledgers, commission &amp; fee settlement.</li>
            <li>Projected-income reporting + a developer executive view across thousands of units and years of timeline.</li>
            <li>This AI assistant — ask it anything about VRX, right here on the site.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="status-row" style="margin-bottom:12px;"><span class="dot soon"></span> Coming soon</div>
          <ul class="ticks" style="--tick:var(--hold);">
            <li><strong style="color:var(--text)">Private alpha</strong> for invited developers and agencies — weeks away.</li>
            <li>The <strong style="color:var(--text)">buyer app</strong> for reserving directly — in build now.</li>
            <li><strong style="color:var(--text)">Developer-local &amp; regional node servers</strong> — distributed scale for global markets.</li>
            <li>Public, vetted marketplace at vettedrealty.com.</li>
            <li>Expansion beyond property into other unique-inventory domains.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:24px;max-width:820px;color:var(--dim);">
        With the right backing, this becomes shared infrastructure that virtually anyone vetted can
        use. If you want to help shape it — as an early developer, an agency, a landowner, or a
        partner — <a href="#contact">now is the moment to get involved</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── GLOSSARY ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="glossary">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Glossary</p>
      <h2>Plain words for every term.</h2>
      <p class="lead" style="max-width:760px;">
        Hover or tap any underlined term anywhere on this page for a quick explanation. Here they all
        are in one place — no jargon left unexplained.
      </p>
      <div class="grid cols-2 reveal" style="margin-top:28px;">
        {GLOSSARY.map((g) => (
          <div class="card" id={`g-${g.key}`}>
            <h3 style="text-transform:capitalize;">{g.term}</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">{g.short}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── CONTACT ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="contact">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Get involved</p>
      <h2>Join the private alpha, or just ask us anything.</h2>
      <p class="lead" style="max-width:720px;">
        Developers, agencies, agents, landowners and partners — tell us a little about you and we'll
        be in touch. An on-page AI assistant to answer questions instantly is on the way.
      </p>

      <div class="grid cols-2" style="margin-top:28px;align-items:start;">
        <form class="card" method="POST" action="./contact.php">
          <label style="display:block;margin-bottom:14px;">
            <span style="display:block;color:var(--dim);font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:6px;">Your name</span>
            <input name="name" required style="width:100%;padding:11px 12px;background:var(--bg2);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:8px;color:var(--text);font-size:1rem;" />
          </label>
          <label style="display:block;margin-bottom:14px;">
            <span style="display:block;color:var(--dim);font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:6px;">Email</span>
            <input name="email" type="email" required style="width:100%;padding:11px 12px;background:var(--bg2);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:8px;color:var(--text);font-size:1rem;" />
          </label>
          <label style="display:block;margin-bottom:14px;">
            <span style="display:block;color:var(--dim);font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:6px;">I am a…</span>
            <select name="role" style="width:100%;padding:11px 12px;background:var(--bg2);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:8px;color:var(--text);font-size:1rem;">
              <option>Property developer</option>
              <option>Agency</option>
              <option>Agent</option>
              <option>Landowner</option>
              <option>Buyer</option>
              <option>Potential partner / investor</option>
              <option>Other</option>
            </select>
          </label>
          <label style="display:block;margin-bottom:18px;">
            <span style="display:block;color:var(--dim);font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:6px;">Message</span>
            <textarea name="message" rows="4" style="width:100%;padding:11px 12px;background:var(--bg2);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:8px;color:var(--text);font-size:1rem;font-family:inherit;"></textarea>
          </label>
          <!-- simple honeypot anti-spam; real submitters leave it blank -->
          <input type="text" name="website" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off" style="position:absolute;left:-9999px;" aria-hidden="true" />
          <button class="btn btn-primary" type="submit">Send</button>
        </form>

        <div>
          <div class="card raised" style="margin-bottom:18px;">
            <h3>Prefer email?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Write to <a href="mailto:vrx@vettedrealty.com">vrx@vettedrealty.com</a> and we'll reply personally.</p>
          </div>
          <!-- Node licenses: the REAL BTC asset layer that funds + runs VRX. Honest framing —
               bought on the open Counterparty (XCP) market, NOT inside VRX; distinct from the in-system $VRX credit. -->
          <div class="card" style="margin-bottom:18px;">
            <h3>Run a node · back the network</h3>
            <p style="margin:0 0 10px;">
              A limited set of <strong style="color:var(--text)">VRX node licenses</strong> is minted on Bitcoin
              (via Counterparty / XCP). Holding one funds the protocol and lets you run a node in the network.
              They trade on the open Counterparty market — not inside VRX.
            </p>
            <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="https://vrx.poolo.org/participate" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">See the node licenses ↗</a>
          </div>
          <div class="card" style="margin-bottom:18px;">
            <h3>Whitepapers</h3>
            <p style="margin:0 0 10px;">Three editions — for judges, for partners/investors, and a technical / protocol deep-dive.</p>
            <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="./whitepapers/">Read the whitepapers ↗</a>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <div class="status-row" style="margin-bottom:10px;"><span class="dot live"></span> Live now</div>
            <h3>Ask the VRX assistant</h3>
            <p style="margin:0 0 14px;">
              An AI assistant is ready to answer anything about how VRX works, what it means for your
              role, or how to join.
            </p>
            <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="./ask.html">✦ Open the assistant</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
